  "prompt": "The attached image is a 216x216 px frame from an animated GIF. Rebuild it by hand as vector art.\n\nTask (2D, static): write an HTML page that reproduces the image as faithfully as you can in inline SVG: characters, poses, facial expressions, hands, props, background, and colors. A later step will animate your SVG, so structure it in named groups.\n- Static only: no <script>, no SMIL (<animate>, <animateTransform>, <animateMotion>, <set>), no CSS animations or transitions.\n\n2D contract:\n- The page body contains a single inline <svg> element with viewBox=\"0 0 216 216\", width=\"216\", and height=\"216\". No nested <svg>, no <foreignObject>, no <canvas>.\n- Put each character in its own <g> with a descriptive id (for example id=\"character-left\"), with nested <g> groups with descriptive ids for parts that could move (head, eyes, mouth, arms, hands, ...).\n- Gradients, clip paths, masks, and SVG filters (for example feGaussianBlur) are allowed.\n\nRules (breaking any rule scores 0):\n- Reply with exactly one ```html code block containing one complete, self-contained HTML document. If you send several blocks, only the last one counts. Do not use tools; answer directly.\n- The page is rendered offline in a 216x216 px viewport at device pixel ratio 1. Fill exactly that area, starting at the top-left corner.\n- No raster images: no <img>, no <image>, no CSS url(...) except url(#id) references to your own SVG definitions, no data: URIs, no base64, no blob: URLs.\n- No external resources or network access: no external scripts, stylesheets, fonts, iframes, links, fetch, XMLHttpRequest, WebSocket, or any other URL.\n- Do not call setTimeout, setInterval, requestAnimationFrame, or requestIdleCallback. The harness controls time.\n- The file must be at most 256 KB and must run without uncaught exceptions or console errors.",
